{#-
  The prover's system prompt for the fixed loop: one CONTINUE(v) of the
  Dream-RSI tree, `sorry` allowed. It states the rules, then the assignment.

  nucleus-prover renders it with minijinja in a strict environment: an
  undefined variable fails the attempt (no model call is made), nothing is
  escaped, there are no includes, a render has a bounded instruction budget
  and a bounded output, and trim_blocks/lstrip_blocks are on (a line holding
  only a {% tag %} leaves no blank line behind). The rendered text is the
  whole system message; the first user message is always "Return the
  complete file.", and the (candidate, verdict) exchanges follow.

  Variables (all always defined):
    mode            "continue"
    problem         the problem id
    statement       the frozen formalization (a complete .lean file)
    file            the parent's workspace
    parent          node being continued (integer), or none
    check           Lean's verdict on `file` or none:
                    {status, invalid_reason, errors: [{line, col, message}],
                     sorries: [{line, col, goal}], errors_total, sorries_total};
                    at most 12 of each kind are listed, each text cut at
                    1500 characters; the *_total counts are the true totals
    siblings        [{node, status, errors, sorries, excerpt}] continuations
                    already made from the same parent, newest 12, excerpts
                    cut likewise
    siblings_total  how many continuations were made from the same parent
    calls_left      model calls this worker may still make, counting this one
    attempt         1-based attempt within this worker

  This template deliberately uses neither `attempt` nor `calls_left`: the
  system message is then identical on every attempt of a worker, so the
  assignment (statement, file, verdict, siblings) is a stable prefix the
  provider can cache. A template that wants them should put them last.
-#}
